<h2 id="section-1">Record the arrangement, not just the material name</h2>
<p>A note saying “titanium earrings” may leave out the back, decorative element, coating and actual product reference. Record the complete piece as far as the seller documents it. Distinguish the seller’s statement from something you have verified independently. A color photograph does not identify an alloy or coating, and a previous comfortable use does not certify every future combination.</p>
<p>Record left and right separately when the observations differ. Keep time approximate if exact timing is not known. “Noticed after putting on a headset” describes a sequence; “the headset caused it” adds an explanation that the note may not support. Clear observations help a professional ask better questions without requiring you to diagnose the situation yourself.</p>
<h2 id="section-4">Use a short, neutral record</h2>
<div class="sj-table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Comparison / Comparaison"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Field</th><th scope="col">Example of a useful entry</th><th scope="col">Avoid turning it into</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Product</td><td>Exact reference and supplied back</td><td>An assumed material from color alone.</td></tr><tr><td>Configuration</td><td>Base and ornament references</td><td>A claim that all similar parts are equivalent.</td></tr><tr><td>Context</td><td>Desk work, scarf, headset or a particular movement</td><td>A confirmed cause based only on timing.</td></tr><tr><td>Observation</td><td>Where and when pressure or another change was noticed</td><td>A diagnosis or severity score copied from strangers.</td></tr><tr><td>Next action</td><td>Product-support question or professional advice sought</td><td>A plan to tolerate symptoms for a longer test.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>You do not need a complex spreadsheet or repeated photographs. A clear short entry is often easier to use than a detailed log you cannot sustain. Keep photographs private and share only relevant information with the professional or seller handling the particular question. There is no need to publish a health history in a product review to obtain assistance.</p>
<h2 id="section-7">Separate product support and health questions</h2>
<p>A seller can clarify the supplied back, reference, surface process or assembly instructions. A maker may assess a damaged part. Those are product questions. A clinician can assess symptoms; an appropriate piercing professional can advise within their competence about a piercing or fitting. Do not ask a shopping assistant to rule out infection or identify an allergy from a photograph.</p>
<p>The NHS advises seeking help promptly if a piercing seems infected. Keep that boundary separate from wardrobe observations. Do not use this article to decide that a symptom is “only pressure,” or to choose treatment. Do not remove jewelry from a suspected infected piercing against medical advice; the appropriate next action is professional guidance, not an experiment with another pair.</p>
<h2 id="section-10">Make the record useful without creating an endurance test</h2>
<p>In a fictional example, a person notes that a familiar earring feels different after a back is replaced. The useful product question concerns the new back’s compatibility and normal fitting. That observation does not establish whether a symptom has a mechanical or material cause. The record preserves the sequence while leaving the explanation open for an appropriate assessment.</p>
<p>Do not deliberately reproduce pain, swap through several unknown materials or keep an irritated piercing under pressure to distinguish hypotheses. A product guide cannot establish a safe challenge protocol for you. If you later consider another piece after appropriate advice, keep its reference and component information so you are comparing documented products rather than category labels.</p>
<h2 id="section-13">Supporting checks for this decision</h2>
<p>The examples below are comparison tools, not SUIJI product prices, certifications or test results. Apply the questions to the actual reference you are considering.</p>
<h3 id="section-15">Component map</h3>
<div class="sj-table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Comparison / Comparaison"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Component</th><th scope="col">Information needed</th><th scope="col">Why it matters</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Post/hook</td><td>exact material, grade, dimensions</td><td>passes through or rests against the ear</td></tr><tr><td>Back</td><td>material, shape, size</td><td>pressure and prolonged contact</td></tr><tr><td>Body/hoop</td><td>base metal, solid/hollow construction</td><td>mass and contact area</td></tr><tr><td>Solder/joint</td><td>material or manufacturing statement</td><td>may differ from body</td></tr><tr><td>Decoration</td><td>stone, glass, resin, adhesive</td><td>weight, care and exposure</td></tr><tr><td>Finish</td><td>plating, PVD, anodising, lacquer</td><td>hides or changes the base surface</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h3 id="section-17">Make a contact map of the earring</h3>
<p><strong>The most useful composition is the one for surfaces that actually touch the ear.</strong> Photograph or sketch the earring from the front, side and back, then assign a material to the post, back, body, solder, connecting ring and decoration.</p>
<p>This exposes over-broad descriptions. An earring may have a sterling-silver body, steel post, different solder and a separately supplied back. Calling the whole object “925 silver” leaves several contact areas unanswered.</p>
<p>For a hoop, include the segment that passes through the hole and the hinge. For a leverback, separate the moving wire, lever and decorative body. For a clip, identify both pinching surfaces and any pad.</p>
<h3 id="section-21">Colour is not composition</h3>
<p><strong>Gold, silver, rose gold and black may describe appearance rather than the base material.</strong> Translate each colour into a process question: solid alloy, plating, PVD, anodising, lacquer or another finish?</p>
<p>Then ask which zones are treated. A gold-coloured body may have an uncoated post or back. A titanium body may receive a deposited coating whose composition and wear need separate evidence. Cleaning instructions should match the weakest component, not the most durable metal named in the title.</p>
<h3 id="section-24">Acceptable, partial and insufficient answers</h3>
<div class="sj-table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Comparison / Comparaison"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Seller answer</th><th scope="col">Assessment</th><th scope="col">Reason</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>“Post and back: specified titanium grade titanium; batch certificate available”</td><td>precise</td><td>material, components, specification and traceability are connected</td></tr><tr><td>“Titanium earring, metal back”</td><td>partial</td><td>rear contact component remains unknown</td></tr><tr><td>“Premium hypoallergenic”</td><td>insufficient</td><td>no composition or evidence</td></tr><tr><td>“Sterling silver”</td><td>partial</td><td>scope, alloying metals, post and solder are unclear</td></tr><tr><td>“Gold PVD”</td><td>insufficient alone</td><td>base metal and coated areas are missing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>A partial answer is the next question, not automatic proof of a bad product.</strong> Contradictory answers between the page, packaging and customer service are more serious and should be resolved before wear.</p>
<h3 id="section-27">Keep a copy of what was promised</h3>
<p><strong>A product page can change after your order.</strong> Save the listing, date, selected variant and any customer-service response with the invoice.</p>
<p>This record helps compare purchases, report inconsistent information and show a doctor or dermatologist the advertised composition if a reaction occurs. It does not prove that the claim was correct, but it documents what the seller represented at the time of sale.</p>
<h3 id="section-30">A reusable specification template</h3>
<blockquote>Post: [exact material/grade]. Back: [exact material]. Body: [material and construction]. Joint/solder: [material if present]. Surface: [process, colour and covered areas]. Decoration: [stone/resin/glue where relevant]. Weight: [g per earring]. Dimensions: [mm]. Evidence: [document, date, sample or lot, scope]. Intended use: [healed lobe/initial piercing/occasional wear].</blockquote>
<p><strong>Leave unknown fields open rather than filling them with assumptions.</strong> This is a record to complete from actual product information, not a finished specification. Keep the seller’s wording when useful, note its source and ask about an unidentified field instead of turning a missing answer into a material claim.</p>
<h3 id="section-33">What if the seller cannot answer?</h3>
<p><strong>Treat the missing field as uncertainty and adjust the intended use.</strong> A decorative earring for brief wear on a healed lobe and initial jewellery placed in a healing wound do not tolerate the same information gap.</p>
<p>Ask one precise follow-up and keep the reply. If the post or back remains unidentified, do not infer its material from the front. If a seller cannot connect a certificate to the product, describe the evidence as unverified rather than calling it false or valid.</p>
<h2 id="section-36">Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3 id="section-37">Can the timing prove a material allergy?</h3>
<p>No. Timing is an observation that may be relevant to a professional, not a diagnosis. A product diary cannot replace an assessment or any testing a clinician considers appropriate.</p>
<h3 id="section-39">Should I log how long I can tolerate a pair?</h3>
<p>Do not make tolerance of discomfort the goal. Note when you noticed a problem and stop the trial. Seek suitable advice rather than trying to extend the duration.</p>
<h3 id="section-41">Must I identify every component before asking for help?</h3>
<p>No. Ask for help when needed with the information you have. Mark unknown fields as unknown; do not delay advice while trying to reconstruct a perfect product record.</p>
<h3 id="section-43">What can I ask SUIJI about?</h3>
<p>Ask about a product reference, documented materials, contents, intended connection or instructions. Health symptoms need an appropriate professional rather than a promise that a different product will resolve them.</p>
